WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.400
so when i showed up in baltimore a
couple of days before july 1st

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.839
i mean it still wasn't crystal clear to
me that i could go

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:13.280
you know two days without sleeping stand
in an or for eight hours at a time

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.119
all those sorts of things now that's one
of the things the sleep deprivation

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:21.279
because people always
obviously i take a lot of flack

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:25.840
because i don't sleep a lot and and the
sleep deprivation

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.240
so what did that low what's that like
when you're going through your residency

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.279
and you're just saying i mean
there's days where you're going 24 48

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:36.399
hours no sleep
and how'd that affect you what did you

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:39.520
think of that what do you think looking
back at it

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.000
what's your opinion of it now well you
know when i decided

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.320
that that i was going to go into surgery
i mean i i always i'm pretty uh insecure

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.480
in general most people don't necessarily
appreciate that on the outside but

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.000
always thinking i don't know if i've got
what it takes here how do i figure it

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.480
out and so my
my whole mo in life is test the system

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.479
hard so when i was in medical school
still deciding this

00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:09.119
i figured well every thursday night i'm
gonna pull an all-nighter

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:12.560
in my room standing up not allowing
myself to eat

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.840
sleep drink pee do anything
and i'm gonna stand at my desk for eight

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.200
hours and practice suturing
you know sort of try to mimic what it

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.520
would be like to be in an operating room
all night

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.960
not being able to go pee not being able
to drink not be able to do this thing

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.799
and so i had a little bit of confidence
go you know i figured okay i can do this

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.240
like i can pull an all-nighter once a
week that's you know not that that's a

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.960
good thing to do but it was like kind of
a confidence thing right

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.920
so then you show up but there's no
preparing you for what you're in store

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.799
for
now i got to point out this is not the

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:47.040
way it is anymore so in 2000

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.159
i want to say like four um
it's almost like labor laws came in

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:57.439
exactly a whole bunch of laws came in
that we could spend hours talking about

00:01:57.439 --> 00:02:00.960
how it turns out the laws haven't
they haven't fixed the underlying root

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.640
problem but nevertheless a whole bunch
of work requirement hours stuff got

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.039
fixed right so you couldn't work more
than 80 hours in a week or 88 depending

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:13.040
on if your program had an exemption
and you couldn't work more than 24 hours

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.239
consecutively and stuff like that
but by the time those laws really kicked

00:02:18.239 --> 00:02:23.680
in i was already gone
and and so i don't really know what it's

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.080
like today but i would
imagine it's um it's

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.200
less demanding from a sleep deprivation
standpoint

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.800
but at the time there were no such rules
and

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.959
you were at the sort of every second
every third or every fourth night call

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:42.160
but then your post call day you would
still work so

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.080
our mutual friend kirk parsley of course
is how we met this is one of his

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.760
favorite stories about me
because he loves this story because it's

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.560
so crazy
so this is july

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.720
of 2001. so i am one month into being a
doc right and i'm on an every third

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.920
night call
rotation at this hospital called bayview

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.360
which is
about five miles from what we call the

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.440
mother ship which is hopkins
and it's out in like east baltimore it's

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.720
in a real crappy part of town not that
there's it really i mean

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.000
i hate saying this but there aren't that
many good parts of town at least back

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.720
then in baltimore
um and uh

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.959
so i'm uh so i show up on a monday at
five o'clock in the morning

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:32.799
so i slept in my own bed sunday night so
monday morning i show up at five

00:03:32.799 --> 00:03:39.280
to round we do our whole thing and
that night i'm supposed to go home so

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.840
that's my off couch called your swing
day

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.120
you show up at five you'll be home by 7
pm that night

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.159
if all goes well so you worked your you
know 14 hours

00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:50.560
and then you sleep in your own bed and
then you get to come back the next day

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.599
and then you're going to be on call
so so we're rounding that afternoon

00:03:55.599 --> 00:04:00.000
so and the uh what the the one of the
senior residents says hey

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.280
so and so didn't show up for their shift
in the er the

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:06.799
the er always staffs one surgical
resident full-time

00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:10.080
and this guy just didn't show up and
she's like and it's a 7

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:17.440
p.m to 7 a.m shift can you cover it
and you know this would be like if you

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.440
asked the most junior guy
you know to do something like he he he'd

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.560
be kicking his own ass to say yes like
he couldn't wait

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.080
to do that right so i was like hell yes
i will do

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.440
that so then i go down to the er and i
work

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.919
all night seven to seven and then the
next morning is now my on call day

00:04:35.919 --> 00:04:42.240
so now i'm 7am cranking away
until that night and then i'm up all

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.360
night on call
so and then it's now wednesday

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.720
and it's my post call day and i'm there
till

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.600
5 or 6 p.m so i've basically been up
from about 4 a.m monday

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:02.560
and it's now 5 or 6 p.m wednesday
and i get in my car to drive home and i

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.440
have to
make it i have to go down this miserable

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:11.440
street called eastern avenue
to hit the 83 to drive up to my place

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.880
and i'm driving along eastern avenue
which is like

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.960
red light after red light after red
light or stop sign or whatever

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.400
and every time i'm at a stop light i
fall asleep

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.759
my foot pops off the clutch and i stall
like i am

00:05:25.759 --> 00:05:31.360
so tired i can't even
you know handle like the gradual you

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.880
know easing off the clutch and the gas
just to move up and up and up i can't do

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:38.320
it
so finally in one moment of lucid

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.199
clarity i'm like
you can't get on the freeway you're

00:05:41.199 --> 00:05:44.560
gonna die
so i was like what's the solution i was

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.880
like you gotta get over and take a nap
so i pull over my car on the side of

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.520
eastern avenue right in front of this
park

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.479
called patterson park which at the time
i don't think i fully understood that

00:05:56.479 --> 00:06:01.360
that was an open air drug traffic market
so i got out but here's the best part

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.400
sorry the logical thing to do would have
been just been stayed in the car and

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.360
take a nap
but i was like you know i haven't seen

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:10.319
the sunlight in like days and it's the
sun is still out like

00:06:10.319 --> 00:06:15.360
i'm gonna go nap in the park so i get
out of the park i'm in green scrubs that

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.479
are covered in
you know blood stains because i was too

00:06:18.479 --> 00:06:23.280
dumb to not change i
go lay down in the park i take my pager

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.880
off and i clip it
to my neck and i set the alarm to like

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.560
go off in one hour
you know so like 7 pm it'll wake me up

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.440
and i'll feel perfectly rested and i'll
be able to drive home

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:37.840
so i lay down in middle of patterson
park and the next thing i know i wake up

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:42.240
it's like
one in the morning two in the morning

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.240
there's needles everywhere there's i
have like

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.520
bites on my arm that look like they're
from rats because they're like nothing

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:52.479
i've ever seen before like these bites
all over me

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:56.400
and i'm just thinking myself like how
did i not get killed here

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.639
like the only thing that prevented me
from dying was how ridiculous the sight

00:07:00.639 --> 00:07:03.120
was
you know if something's so strange like

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:07.360
nothing you know so
i was like god damn it man so i get back

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:10.560
in my car and drove home and
when i tell parsley that story he loves

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.160
it because he's like that's the classic
example of how

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:17.440
complete deprivation of sleep impairs
your judgment

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:21.199
um so you know there were a couple
stories in residency where i had to do

00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:23.360
like those three night back to back to
backs

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.520
another time it happened i remember was
an o5 do you feel like though okay

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:31.680
driving a car is kind of boring right
stop lights but when you're really tired

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.199
but then all of a sudden something
happens like

00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:37.280
boom i feel like when that happens i
feel i can lock on

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.000
there's almost no amount of sleep
deprivation that will stop me from

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.919
functioning and getting something done
that's important

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:45.120
on task on time absolutely is that true
does that mean

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.080
yeah i know i think i think an
adrenaline rush in the moment can

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.440
provide any amount
of clarity and focus that's necessary

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.160
the problem is very few things are life
or death that way

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.039
now in your world there were plenty of
things but in my world not and this is

00:07:57.039 --> 00:08:03.440
the actual story i was gonna tell was
in uh probably my third year

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.879
uh i think it was my third year um
i was the same same sort of deal i ended

00:08:08.879 --> 00:08:11.599
up covering for somebody and then
covering for two people and two

00:08:11.599 --> 00:08:13.440
consecutive
nights and then it was my turn so that

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.080
was like three straight nights of not
sleeping

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.840
and it's the middle of the night and
this guy comes in the er who's

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:23.440
you know got a really diseased
gallbladder and in retrospect it should

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:27.039
have never come out that night but
a lot of times the dirty little secret

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:29.840
is surgeons want to operate at night
because they can get better or

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:33.760
time electively as opposed to waiting
the next day if it's not emergent so

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:37.279
sure enough we're in the middle of the
operating room doing a laparoscopic

00:08:37.279 --> 00:08:40.320
gallbladder removal
at two in the morning when we don't

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.640
really need to be there two in the
morning we could have

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:48.880
slept and done it the next day um
and sure enough so so in a lab removing

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.880
the gallbladder is a two surgeon
procedure

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:54.000
so there's the main surgeon which
because we're teaching hospital i'm the

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.720
main surgeon i'm actually doing the
operation and then there's the attending

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:00.720
who's holding the camera and retracting
for me so i'm you know like i'm doing

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:03.839
this
and yeah that's a stimulating activity

00:09:03.839 --> 00:09:07.200
but of course i've done a hundred of
them so it's not that stimulating

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.720
well i can't stay awake and i finally
somehow managed to fall asleep on this

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.519
patient like i
face plant into the patient wow luckily

00:09:17.519 --> 00:09:20.240
it was laparoscopic so i didn't
contaminate the surgical

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.800
but so even something as stimulating as
surgery

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:28.000
right because look if the guy's aorta
was bleeding right right

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.120
i'm sure i would have guessed that yeah
exactly but all you're doing is falling

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:34.160
out
yeah bladder is no big thing yeah yeah

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:37.440
it wasn't stimulating enough to overcome
three days of sleep debt

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.480
did you do did you figure out any tricks
any

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:44.080
you know for instance i can't when i was
going through seal training and even my

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:47.680
whole career and even to this day
i take these i take little power naps if

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.600
i'm feeling that tired
i elevate my feet and i sleep for like

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.440
six to eight minutes
and it totally recharges me did you do

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:00.160
you take power naps
no aside from your power now there's a

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.600
park that lasted seven hours it almost
got you killed

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:07.680
i do i once took a power nap in my car
again same thing i was like

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:10.800
it was like eight o'clock at night i got
to the pool

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:14.000
i had a rule which was after you left
the hospital you never went home

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.440
because i knew if i went home i wasn't
gonna leave so i would always go from

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.399
the hospital
to the gym or the pool or whatever i was

00:10:20.399 --> 00:10:23.440
doing and on this particular night i get
there it's eight o'clock at night

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:27.440
i am so tired i'm nauseous like the
thought so you didn't want to go home

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:29.839
just because you knew you'd go
don't go to sleep and you didn't want to

00:10:29.839 --> 00:10:32.160
go to sleep because you've been awake
for three days why didn't you

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.560
why did you want to go and fall asleep
well because i you know it's really

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:38.079
funny a good friend of mine who again
a couple years ahead of me gave me

00:10:38.079 --> 00:10:41.279
interesting advice whether it was right
or wrong i don't know he said look

00:10:41.279 --> 00:10:44.720
you're going to be tired no matter what
in residency

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:49.120
don't stop doing all the other things in
your life because you'll still be tired

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.240
now i don't know that was the right
advice but i took it i heard that advice

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:54.800
and
my view was i'm going to continue to

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:58.320
work out every day i'm not on call
i'm going to you know do all the stuff i

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.399
want to do and unfortunately sleep
always took the brunt of it

00:11:02.399 --> 00:11:05.519
so yeah so it would be to the pool and
so same thing i got there but on this

00:11:05.519 --> 00:11:10.000
particular night i was so
tired i was so nauseous like i couldn't

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:13.279
suppress the desire to vomit
and i was like i'm not gonna i can't if

00:11:13.279 --> 00:11:16.160
i puke in the pool they'll kick me out i
won't get to swim it's just not gonna be

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:21.120
worth it so i was like
just sit here for like 30 minutes

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:24.240
and take a quick nap so same thing took
the pager out which normally could wake

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.959
me up
clip it here and it was freezing because

00:11:26.959 --> 00:11:32.079
it was like the winter so i
left no it wasn't cold but i remember i

00:11:32.079 --> 00:11:36.320
wanted to leave the radio on
like a dumb ass so the car is off so

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:39.279
alternator's off
radio's on well you know how the story

00:11:39.279 --> 00:11:42.240
ends i wake up at two in the morning
battery's dead i'm in the middle of the

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:46.320
parking lot i can't you know
had to call my girlfriend to get jumper

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:49.680
cables to get me out
that's a one that girlfriend went on to

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:54.880
become my wife by the way
um so short answer no i didn't have a

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.600
great strategy i did have
a friend who and i never tried this but

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:03.600
he told me he would put coffee grounds
in his eyelids and apparently it hurts

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:07.600
so much that you couldn't close your
eyes

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:11.839
you know that's i mean i would not that
but i've told people this all the time

00:12:11.839 --> 00:12:14.320
too you know do
do a little bit of physical exercise if

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:18.399
you're super tired that definitely
picks you back up there's got to be some

00:12:18.399 --> 00:12:21.200
reality or some scientific reason for
that

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:25.839
happening that's another thing you can
do

